# Two countries, one Gini
subtitle: "How the Lorenz curve turns into the Gini coefficient, and why two very different countries can share the same Gini."

## Two countries, one Gini
use: lorenz_curve
with: {"gini":0.27,"shade":true}
adjust:
    label_equality:
      scale: 1.5
    gini_caption:
      scale: 1.5
sources:
    - id: lorenz1905
      title: Methods of Measuring the Concentration of Wealth
      authors: Max O. Lorenz
      year: 1905
      finding: Introduced the Lorenz curve.
    - id: gini1912
      title: Variabilità e mutabilità
      authors: Corrado Gini
      year: 1912
      finding: Introduced the concentration ratio now called the Gini coefficient.

    polygon triangle dashed #8f887c points [[210,120],[760,120],[760,630]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    math gini_def tex "\\text{Gini} = \\frac{\\text{shaded}}{\\text{triangle}}" x 350 y 560 hidden true
    path curve_b smooth #8a5fa8 points [[210,120],[237.5,135.9],[265,151.7],[292.5,167.6],[320,183.5],[347.5,199.4],[375,215.3],[402.5,231.4],[430,247.7],[457.5,264.3],[485,281.6],[512.5,299.8],[540,319.3],[567.5,340.7],[595,364.7],[622.5,392.2],[650,424.3],[677.5,462.3],[705,507.9],[732.5,563.1],[760,630]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text label_a "Country A" #b5482e x 470 y 188 font_size 28 hidden true
    text label_b "Country B" #8a5fa8 x 690 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true
    text gini_b "B: Gini = 0.27 too" #8a5fa8 x 340 y 480 font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait portrait_corrado_gini cites ["gini1912"] x 105 y 430 width 140 of "Corrado Gini" cameo true hidden true
    polygon square dashed #c9c2b6 points [[210,120],[760,120],[760,630],[210,630]] style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true
    dot pA50 #b5482e at.x 485 at.y 273 hidden true
    text tA50 "30%" #b5482e x 530 y 250 font_size 26 hidden true
    dot pB40 #8a5fa8 at.x 430 at.y 247.5 hidden true
    dot pA40 #b5482e at.x 430 at.y 223.6 hidden true
    text h40 "poorest 40%" #8f887c x 880 y 330 font_size 26 hidden true
    text b40 "B 25%" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 295 font_size 26 hidden true
    text a40 "A 20%" #b5482e x 880 y 260 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Two countries, one Gini"

    hide label_lorenz

The Gini coefficient squeezes a country's whole income distribution into one number. To see what it keeps and what it drops, start with a curve.
    draw axes

Line everyone up from poorest to richest, and plot the share of all income held by each share of the people. With equal incomes, that is this diagonal.
    draw equality label_equality

A real distribution sags below it. In country A, the poorer half of people get 30 percent of the income, not 50.
    draw lorenz label_a

Here: half the people, 30 percent of the income.
    draw pA50 tA50

Everything below the diagonal makes up this triangle, half of the square.
    draw square triangle

The Gini is the share of that triangle lying between the diagonal and the curve.
    draw gini_area gini_def

For A it is 0.27. Zero would be perfect equality; one, a single person holding everything.
    draw gini_caption

    erase pA50 tA50

Now country B. Its poorest 40 percent get 25 percent of the income, against 20 percent in A.
    draw curve_b label_b pB40 pA40 h40 b40 a40

But its richest tenth take 24 percent of the income, against 17 in A. The two curves cross.
    dot pB90 #8a5fa8 at.x 705 at.y 507.9
    dot pA90 #b5482e at.x 705 at.y 544
    text h90 "richest 10%" #8f887c x 880 y 580 font_size 26
    text b90 "B 24%" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 545 font_size 26
    text a90 "A 17%" #b5482e x 880 y 510 font_size 26

And B's Gini is also 0.27. What B's curve gains at the bottom it loses at the top, and the areas balance exactly.
    draw gini_b

So which country is more unequal? When curves cross, it depends on whether you care more about the bottom or the top, and one number has chosen for you.
    highlight curve_b lorenz

Max Lorenz drew his curve in 1905, as a graduate student.
    highlight lorenz

Corrado Gini turned the idea into a single number in 1912.
    draw portrait_corrado_gini

    erase portrait_corrado_gini

So a Gini is a fair summary only when curves do not cross. Otherwise, quote it with a share at each end.
    highlight b40 a40 b90 a90

    quiz "Two countries both have a Gini of 0.35. What follows?"
        right: Nothing definite. Equal areas can come from curves that cross, one kinder to the bottom, the other richer at the top.
        * Their poorest 40 percent get the same share
        + Nothing definite about any group's share
        * Their Lorenz curves are identical
---
